WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.740 --> 00:00:03.350
Okay, let’s talk about The Wall.

00:00:03.350 --> 00:00:07.640
The Wall is a multimedia project created by
English rock band Pink Floyd, consisting of

00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:15.769
a 1979 studio album, a live performance touring
over 1980 and 81, a 1982 experimental film,

00:00:15.769 --> 00:00:22.060
a 2010 through 2013 revival tour, a 2015 concert
film, and a 2016 opera.

00:00:22.060 --> 00:00:26.460
The plot of all the various adaptations center
around a rock star named Pink who struggles

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:28.710
with depression and substance abuse.

00:00:28.710 --> 00:00:33.380
In the midst of an emotional spiral Pink reflects
on/hallucinates the events of his life that

00:00:33.380 --> 00:00:38.480
have led to that point, including vivid fabrication
of the death of his father in World War 2,

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:42.750
hazy memories of The Blitz, his relationship
with his mother, creative dissatisfaction,

00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:47.260
a decaying marriage, and a job in the music
industry where he is treated as a commodity

00:00:47.260 --> 00:00:52.260
to be used, all of which form the metaphorical
bricks in the emotional wall that Pink uses

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:54.710
to separate himself from the world.

00:00:54.710 --> 00:01:00.300
I want to focus in mostly on the 1982 film,
because it is, I think, the adaptation that

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:02.790
is able to do the most with the concept.

00:01:02.790 --> 00:01:07.420
The film was written by Pink Floyd frontman
Roger Waters, directed by Alan Parker, with

00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:12.990
animation by Gerald Scarfe, and it is, well,
it’s a pretty heavy film.

00:01:12.990 --> 00:01:14.820
It can be a hard watch.

00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:20.090
It comes from a very dark place, emotionally
speaking, there’s a lot of disturbing imagery.

00:01:20.090 --> 00:01:22.659
It’s a very angry film.

00:01:22.659 --> 00:01:29.850
[singing] When we grew up and went to school
there were certain teachers who would hurt

00:01:29.850 --> 00:01:33.939
the children any way they could.

00:01:33.939 --> 00:01:38.850
It’s pretty abstract in its presentation,
relying heavily on associative imagery rather

00:01:38.850 --> 00:01:44.139
than conventional narrative, and this is both
a strength and a weakness for the film.

00:01:44.139 --> 00:01:47.531
Abstraction is, counter-intuitively, very
efficient.

00:01:47.531 --> 00:01:54.399
It allows a movie to be about a lot of things
simultaneously by letting symbols bleed into

00:01:54.399 --> 00:01:55.399
one another.

00:01:55.399 --> 00:02:00.600
The movie pinballs between war, classism,
police brutality, the criminalization of youth

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:05.640
culture, Thatcherism, generational trauma,
commodification of art, and the home-grown

00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:09.869
fascism of the nations that bragged about
beating the Nazis, and it’s able to do this

00:02:09.869 --> 00:02:12.000
pretty fluidly.

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:18.160
The film’s underlying anger is, true to
form, often incoherent, struggling to vocalize

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:23.880
and manifest the exact shape of the subject,
falling back on abstract imagery in order

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:28.510
to suggest an emotional truth where literalism
is insufficient.

00:02:28.510 --> 00:02:32.550
This allows the film to create really powerful
moments.

00:02:32.550 --> 00:02:38.540
A running motif is anti-war, expressed as
a seething rage of betrayal, abandonment,

00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:40.890
and exploitation at the hands of the system.

00:02:40.890 --> 00:02:45.860
Roger Waters’ father was a conscientious
objector during World War 2, a pacifist who

00:02:45.860 --> 00:02:47.780
drove ambulance during The Blitz.

00:02:47.780 --> 00:02:52.800
In 1943, however, he changed his mind and
was commissioned as a Second Lieutenant in

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:56.210
the Territorial Army in September of that
year.

00:02:56.210 --> 00:02:58.210
He was dead five months later.

00:02:58.210 --> 00:03:02.220
In ‘Goodbye Blue Skies’ the Union Jack
shatters into pieces.

00:03:02.220 --> 00:03:08.180
For all its bravado and blustre, English nationalism
is just blood in the gutter.

00:03:08.180 --> 00:03:13.370
Soldiers sent to war don’t die for their
country, they just die.

00:03:13.370 --> 00:03:18.340
The boys beat the Germans and came home just
in time to build atom bombs.

00:03:18.340 --> 00:03:23.870
Symbols shift and merge and break apart, juxtaposed
and contrasted in order to create an impression

00:03:23.870 --> 00:03:28.990
of their interconnected relationship in a
way that is difficult to do using mere words

00:03:28.990 --> 00:03:35.450
and, core to the theme of the piece as a whole,
no one specific thing is the load-bearing

00:03:35.450 --> 00:03:36.740
cause.

00:03:36.740 --> 00:03:41.980
There is no singular source of the sickness
in society that isolates us from one another,

00:03:41.980 --> 00:03:45.510
that suppresses us, that brutalizes us, that
exploits us.

00:03:45.510 --> 00:03:48.520
They are all just bricks in the wall.

00:03:48.520 --> 00:03:52.010
This abstraction, though, can also work against
the film.

00:03:52.010 --> 00:03:58.070
A lot of the film’s imagery, almost forty
years later, has an almost juvenile Makes-U-Think

00:03:58.070 --> 00:04:03.240
shallowness to it, worn down by a generation
of trite commentary on the relationship between

00:04:03.240 --> 00:04:09.790
art and commerce, in no small part directly
influenced by The Wall, that cheapens The

00:04:09.790 --> 00:04:12.300
Wall simply by association.

00:04:12.300 --> 00:04:17.120
The lack of a concise resolution, the lack
of a clear point at the end, which functions

00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:23.060
for some viewers as a powerfully disquieting
denial of closure also leaves the door open

00:04:23.060 --> 00:04:28.030
for symbols to take on meanings counter to
their intended or desired use.

00:04:28.030 --> 00:04:32.659
A centerpiece moment in the film comes when
Pink, suffering an overdose, is revived by

00:04:32.659 --> 00:04:38.160
his manager and record industry flunkies in
order to be dressed and pushed out on stage.

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:44.090
In this state Pink conceptualizes himself
in an alter-ego, a fascist dictator indoctrinating

00:04:44.090 --> 00:04:49.659
teens, primed by a conformist education system,
into hate and violence.

00:04:49.659 --> 00:04:54.810
This sequence, spanning the songs In The Flesh,
Run Like Hell, and Waiting for the Worms,

00:04:54.810 --> 00:04:58.080
includes dramatization of hate crimes and
sexual assault.

00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:03.100
The film’s imagery of crossed hammers, invented
for the movie as a deliberately unsettling

00:05:03.100 --> 00:05:08.870
expression of contemporary fascism and its
ability to utilize the banal, the blunt, the

00:05:08.870 --> 00:05:15.430
simplistic, was adopted in 1988 by actual
neo-Nazi group the Hammerskins, a now fractured

00:05:15.430 --> 00:05:20.800
organization whose dispersed members remain
active in other hate organizations to this

00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:21.840
day.

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:27.740
The potent imagery, structured as a condemnation,
was still adopted by people who saw the violence

00:05:27.740 --> 00:05:31.379
not as horrifying but as aspirational.

00:05:31.379 --> 00:05:37.200
The character of Pink holds fears and resentments
towards women that are emotionally challenging

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:42.110
to watch expressed on screen and while a mature
audience can certainly parse the difference

00:05:42.110 --> 00:05:47.039
between a character’s excuses and reality,
the film’s lack of closure means there’s

00:05:47.039 --> 00:05:51.889
little guidance on what the audience is ultimately
meant to take away from the depictions of

00:05:51.889 --> 00:05:56.389
Pink being devoured by a predatory, vaginal
flower.

00:05:56.389 --> 00:06:00.590
And part of that is rooted in the film’s
emotional rawness, the dark place of the psyche

00:06:00.590 --> 00:06:06.430
where thoughts and fears and beliefs are not
rational but are deeply potent and extremely

00:06:06.430 --> 00:06:07.430
present.

00:06:07.430 --> 00:06:12.840
Pink intellectually understands that his own
self-destruction and alienation drove his

00:06:12.840 --> 00:06:19.169
wife away, but emotionally still conceptualizes
her as a consuming predator.

00:06:19.169 --> 00:06:26.470
It doesn’t all make sense or resolve or
coalesce because we don’t all make sense.

00:06:26.470 --> 00:06:30.940
Everyone is the illusion of order constructed,
brick by brick, out of chaos.

00:06:30.940 --> 00:06:36.360
So, while The Wall is far from flawless and
certainly not for everyone, there are still,

00:06:36.360 --> 00:06:42.319
I feel, deep emotional truths in there, a
human story about the contradiction of being,

00:06:42.319 --> 00:06:48.229
the self-amplifying spiral of isolation, and
the fragility and hypocrisy of power.

00:06:48.229 --> 00:06:52.050
The album is good, but it was always written
with a visual component in mind, be it the

00:06:52.050 --> 00:06:58.839
concert or the film, and I think the album
definitely feels less than whole as a result.

00:06:58.839 --> 00:07:02.930
The movie also adds ‘When the Tigers Broke
Free’, which is a song that I think adds

00:07:02.930 --> 00:07:04.680
a lot of welcome depth to the story.

00:07:04.680 --> 00:07:09.499
It’s a really potent encapsulation of how
Waters feels about the system that killed

00:07:09.499 --> 00:07:11.409
his father.

00:07:11.409 --> 00:07:21.919
[singing] And that’s how the high command
took my daddy from me

00:07:21.919 --> 00:07:28.160
And this is a movie that Doug Walker, a man
whose bread and butter is getting irrationally

00:07:28.160 --> 00:07:35.779
angry at movies for 9 year olds, decided to
riff in the form of a parody recreation.

00:07:35.779 --> 00:07:45.539
[singing] Good old Pink Floyd made a movie
devoid of even the slightest bit of subtlety.

00:07:45.539 --> 00:07:50.379
Okay, so, what is this?

00:07:50.379 --> 00:07:52.969
Why does this exist?

00:07:52.969 --> 00:07:59.020
I’m going to have to say a bunch of unkind
things, and there’s really no way around

00:07:59.020 --> 00:08:03.419
that, because the observable facts are just,
prima facia, unkind.

00:08:03.419 --> 00:08:08.419
[Dracula voice] Well, maybe it’s the other
way alound, I don’t know, I’ve got a high

00:08:08.419 --> 00:08:09.719
school education!

00:08:09.719 --> 00:08:10.719
Muahahahah!

00:08:10.719 --> 00:08:13.629
This is pandering like hell

00:08:13.629 --> 00:08:17.319
Basically it’s impossible to engage with
something like The Wall, even sarcastically

00:08:17.319 --> 00:08:21.860
or disingenuously, without leaving a lot of
yourself on the table as a result.

00:08:21.860 --> 00:08:26.710
What you fixate on, what you think is silly,
why you think it’s silly, these are all

00:08:26.710 --> 00:08:28.750
revealing.

00:08:28.750 --> 00:08:34.789
And that’s not to say that you need to love
The Wall or even like The Wall or, you know,

00:08:34.789 --> 00:08:39.860
you’re a phoney or a poser or shallow if
you don’t, it’s just that defending that

00:08:39.860 --> 00:08:43.039
position is going to demand emotional honesty.

00:08:43.039 --> 00:08:47.910
For most critics that’s not a problem, but,
we’ll see.

00:08:47.910 --> 00:08:53.710
The Nostalgia Critic, AKA The Critic, AKA
That Guy With The Glasses is the brainchild

00:08:53.710 --> 00:08:58.839
of Doug Walker, an aspiring filmmaker from the suburbs of Chicago who, in 2008, stumbled

00:08:58.839 --> 00:09:04.220
into an early online video success with the
then-nascent genre of angry reviews.

00:09:04.220 --> 00:09:08.899
The basic format, more or less unchanged in
13 years, involves a recap of the plot of

00:09:08.899 --> 00:09:14.360
a film interspersed with one-liners and skits,
usually with the comedy predicated on slapstick

00:09:14.360 --> 00:09:19.230
reactions of either extreme confusion or extreme
anger or both.

00:09:19.230 --> 00:09:20.230
Seven million.

00:09:20.230 --> 00:09:22.529
Never leave the cave without it.

00:09:22.529 --> 00:09:25.280
A bat credit card?

00:09:25.280 --> 00:09:29.990
They gave him a bat credit card?

00:09:29.990 --> 00:09:39.040
They had the BALLS to give one of THE GREATEST
SUPER HEROES OF ALL TIME A BAT CREDIT CARD?!

00:09:39.040 --> 00:09:43.860
In September 2019 they published Nostalgia
Critic’s The Wall, a 40 minute musical review

00:09:43.860 --> 00:09:48.730
featuring musician Corey Taylor, his son Griff
Taylor, and the collaborative works of musician

00:09:48.730 --> 00:09:50.880
Rob Scallon, and animator Fennah.

00:09:50.880 --> 00:09:53.709
The reception was… not good.

00:09:53.709 --> 00:09:59.130
[singing] all in all it’s just not very
funny at all

00:09:59.130 --> 00:10:04.510
No exaggeration, I’m not being facetious,
I’m not trying to be a dick.

00:10:04.510 --> 00:10:07.810
This is the worst movie review I have ever
seen.

00:10:07.810 --> 00:10:13.490
When I first tried to watch Doug Walker’s
The Wall this is what happened to my computer,

00:10:13.490 --> 00:10:15.690
so clearly it was trying to protect me.

00:10:15.690 --> 00:10:21.860
This review has become somewhat notorious
online because there is something deeply compelling

00:10:21.860 --> 00:10:23.060
about it.

00:10:23.060 --> 00:10:31.209
[singing] The art-house ego-trip had fallen
and slipped to the bargain big for $3.93

00:10:31.209 --> 00:10:36.589
There is a tremendous amount of effort on
display, but it’s also really lazy, if that

00:10:36.589 --> 00:10:37.800
makes any sense.

00:10:37.800 --> 00:10:43.860
The priority really seems to be in doing a
lot, doing everything, rather than doing any

00:10:43.860 --> 00:10:46.300
of it particularly well.

00:10:46.300 --> 00:10:48.009
And this is what’s confusing.

00:10:48.009 --> 00:10:55.839
There’s so much going on, so many collaborations,
so many moving parts, that it clearly took

00:10:55.839 --> 00:11:01.720
time and energy and passion, an energy and
passion that needs to be maintained on some

00:11:01.720 --> 00:11:06.890
level for the weeks and weeks that it takes
to put all this together, but there’s no

00:11:06.890 --> 00:11:11.820
evidence, whatsoever, of what was actually
driving that.

00:11:11.820 --> 00:11:15.709
You don’t just make something like this
on accident in an afternoon.

00:11:15.709 --> 00:11:20.029
This would have taken weeks, if not months,
to coordinate.

00:11:20.029 --> 00:11:24.579
The collaborations with Satellite City and
Rob Scallon needed to be arranged, Corey Taylor

00:11:24.579 --> 00:11:30.380
and his son needed to come out to Chicago,
the final version of The Trial probably took

00:11:30.380 --> 00:11:35.250
eighty hours just to render, not even including
blocking and animating.

00:11:35.250 --> 00:11:41.149
Then there’s writing, recording, props,
costumes, all of which you can cut a lot of

00:11:41.149 --> 00:11:48.639
corners on, and make no mistake the corners
here are extremely cut, but there’s a floor.

00:11:48.639 --> 00:11:52.620
Certain things are still going to take all
week no matter how half you ass them.

00:11:52.620 --> 00:12:00.290
So on some level you have to care, you have
to care a lot to commit to this amount of

00:12:00.290 --> 00:12:01.290
work.

00:12:01.290 --> 00:12:08.649
And that passion, you know, it can come from
just really loving The Wall, or just really

00:12:08.649 --> 00:12:14.970
hating The Wall, or feeling like you have
a lot of stuff to say about it, but it’s

00:12:14.970 --> 00:12:16.550
gotta come from somewhere.

00:12:16.550 --> 00:12:21.610
In the middle Doug does an ad for the companion
album, that they were selling – for money

00:12:21.610 --> 00:12:27.010
– that Doug describes as a love letter to
Pink Floyd and their music, and it’s just…

00:12:27.010 --> 00:12:31.709
This is just such a love letter to Pink Floyd’s
music and The Wall, and you can download the

00:12:31.709 --> 00:12:33.560
album in the link below.

00:12:33.560 --> 00:12:39.890
so hollow as a pitch because the product,
the thing that we’re watching and listening

00:12:39.890 --> 00:12:40.890
to,

00:12:40.890 --> 00:12:42.740
[singing] the art-house ego-trip

00:12:42.740 --> 00:12:49.420
is extremely clearly not a love letter to
Pink Floyd, and is dominated by someone who,

00:12:49.420 --> 00:12:53.730
based on the words being said, doesn’t seem
to know much about Pink Floyd at all in the

00:12:53.730 --> 00:12:56.870
first place, and really doesn’t like what
they do know.

00:12:56.870 --> 00:13:01.870
[singing] it’s us vs. them, I don’t even
know who us or them are, I just want to be

00:13:01.870 --> 00:13:03.460
angry so I can be Tweeting

00:13:03.460 --> 00:13:10.190
Doug doesn’t seem to understand The Wall
on even a surface level, and really just doesn’t

00:13:10.190 --> 00:13:17.690
appear to care much about The Wall one way
or another, sort of?

00:13:17.690 --> 00:13:23.520
There is at no point any sense that Doug has
any personal relationship to the material.

00:13:23.520 --> 00:13:27.730
Based off the depth of Doug’s commentary
it reads very much like the opinions of someone

00:13:27.730 --> 00:13:33.750
who watched The Wall for the first time after
already committing to do a full recreation,

00:13:33.750 --> 00:13:39.170
and then didn’t particularly enjoy it, and
wasn’t really able to digest it because

00:13:39.170 --> 00:13:44.129
the whole viewing went through the filter
of looking for ideas for the recreation.

00:13:44.129 --> 00:13:51.920
The commentary is extremely shallow while
also being deeply petty and mean, but then

00:13:51.920 --> 00:13:56.439
also the longer you go into the review the
further it drifts from even being about The

00:13:56.439 --> 00:14:01.870
Wall and the more it becomes a showcase for
Doug’s own unrelated opinions.

00:14:01.870 --> 00:14:06.610
There’s no clear sense of what viewpoints
the various characters are supposed to represent,

00:14:06.610 --> 00:14:12.550
The Critic is used to voice multiple different
stances, being both critic, audience surrogate,

00:14:12.550 --> 00:14:18.790
creator surrogate, narrator, and protagonist,
but then all of those are caked in various

00:14:18.790 --> 00:14:24.629
layers of sarcasm that make it really difficult
to parse what the message is actually supposed

00:14:24.629 --> 00:14:25.629
to be.

00:14:25.629 --> 00:14:28.090
The whole of it is just very unclear.

00:14:28.090 --> 00:14:32.889
The commentary, when it’s not completely
off topic, waffles between faint praise like

00:14:32.889 --> 00:14:38.439
“well, I guess the song is kinda rad”
and really performatively cruel stuff, like

00:14:38.439 --> 00:14:41.440
calling the creators clout-chasing fakes.

00:14:41.440 --> 00:14:45.389
None of it feels like the kind of good-natured
ribbing you’d get from actually invested

00:14:45.389 --> 00:14:48.560
fans who are making fun of a thing that they
love.

00:14:48.560 --> 00:14:52.490
What’s missing, at the core, is any sense
of motivation.

00:14:52.490 --> 00:14:54.269
That’s it?

00:14:54.269 --> 00:14:56.170
We’re stopping there?

00:14:56.170 --> 00:14:58.470
Well, yeah, the movie did.

00:14:58.470 --> 00:15:00.470
I didn’t even get a line.

00:15:00.470 --> 00:15:02.310
Hell, I didn’t even sing a song.

00:15:02.310 --> 00:15:04.139
What’d you even think of the movie?

00:15:04.139 --> 00:15:08.819
Well, the movie ended with such an open vagueness
that it only makes sense the review end on

00:15:08.819 --> 00:15:10.830
such an open vagueness.

00:15:10.830 --> 00:15:16.690
You get to the end and have no idea why this
was the form this product should take.

00:15:16.690 --> 00:15:21.090
Why make a straight reproduction of pretty
much 50% of the entire film?

00:15:21.090 --> 00:15:23.339
Why make a complete companion album?

00:15:23.339 --> 00:15:29.660
Why put this much effort into a video about
a subject that you just don’t seem to have

00:15:29.660 --> 00:15:32.029
any substantive opinions on at all?

00:15:32.029 --> 00:15:38.220
And why put all this effort into this concept
for this specific movie?

00:15:38.220 --> 00:15:39.220
I liked it fine.

00:15:39.220 --> 00:15:40.220
Yeah, me too.

00:15:40.220 --> 00:15:42.269
A little full of itself, but good music and
imagination.

00:15:42.269 --> 00:15:43.269
Fair enough.

00:15:43.269 --> 00:15:48.220
And having these questions afterwards is very
bizarre when you’re talking about a review,

00:15:48.220 --> 00:15:54.649
something that is, theoretically at least,
predicated entirely on, ya know, talking about

00:15:54.649 --> 00:15:56.019
your opinions.

00:15:56.019 --> 00:16:00.139
And this is where we need to accept the reality
that this isn’t really a review, it’s

00:16:00.139 --> 00:16:04.040
not a parody, it’s not a commentary, and
it’s not a spoof.

00:16:04.040 --> 00:16:06.579
It’s barely even about The Wall.

00:16:06.579 --> 00:16:10.390
It’s honestly closer to fanfiction, in a
way?

00:16:10.390 --> 00:16:15.749
Doug wants to be a filmmaker, he wants to
make art, but he can’t, because he’s a

00:16:15.749 --> 00:16:20.269
fundamentally incurious person who isn’t
much interested in what other people think

00:16:20.269 --> 00:16:26.009
or feel, and all his ideas boil down to “what
if Batman met Mario?”

00:16:26.009 --> 00:16:30.069
Slapstick focuses on the most important rule
of comedy, that all comedy is based in one

00:16:30.069 --> 00:16:33.680
way or another on misery.

00:16:33.680 --> 00:16:39.560
[teleporter noise] [fanfare]

00:16:39.560 --> 00:16:44.740
With little to no dialogue, quickly identifiable
characters, and nothing but chases.

00:16:44.740 --> 00:16:49.569
People see it as classic because it’s so
minimal, and yet so interesting.

00:16:49.569 --> 00:16:52.649
It says so much with just a few clever choices.

00:16:52.649 --> 00:16:55.060
‘Fury Road’ is the same way.

00:16:55.060 --> 00:17:00.209
He is YouTube’s own home grown Tommy Wiseau
or Neil Breen, complete with the same unwillingness

00:17:00.209 --> 00:17:01.779
to learn how a camera works.

00:17:01.779 --> 00:17:05.510
Hey, so you invited me up here, what do you
want to do?

00:17:05.510 --> 00:17:06.510
Just watch TV.

00:17:06.510 --> 00:17:08.970
You invited me up here to literally watch
TV?

00:17:08.970 --> 00:17:12.490
I’m a successful musician with millions,
how can you expect me to be happy?

00:17:12.490 --> 00:17:18.959
It has that same heady mix of incompetence
and ego that is absolutely intoxicating to

00:17:18.959 --> 00:17:19.990
talk about.

00:17:19.990 --> 00:17:25.409
So much of the actual content is predicated
on readings of the material that are just

00:17:25.409 --> 00:17:30.400
wildly wrong, and yet so absolutely confident
in their delivery.

00:17:30.400 --> 00:17:36.240
Doug decided to accuse an eighty minute movie
of being too long and padded, and then didn’t

00:17:36.240 --> 00:17:41.029
even flinch as he sandwiched that between
a rant about people being mean to him on Twitter

00:17:41.029 --> 00:17:44.820
and an eight minute argument with a busty
nightmare squirrel.

00:17:44.820 --> 00:17:49.920
And it’s that element, that deeply misplaced
sense of confidence that really just makes

00:17:49.920 --> 00:17:52.860
Nostalgia Critic’s The Wall captivating.

00:17:52.860 --> 00:17:58.480
Like The Room it’s a deeply cynical product
made by a fundamentally untalented man, bored

00:17:58.480 --> 00:18:03.720
by the idea of personal growth, building so
many layers of self-protective irony that

00:18:03.720 --> 00:18:06.010
it becomes unintentionally revealing.

00:18:06.010 --> 00:18:07.010
You mean like ‘The Room’?

00:18:07.010 --> 00:18:08.010
No.

00:18:08.010 --> 00:18:09.010
Yes.

00:18:09.010 --> 00:18:10.010
Shut up.

00:18:10.010 --> 00:18:13.529
Doug is utilizing The Wall as a showcase for
his own ideas, his own lyricism, his own jokes,

00:18:13.529 --> 00:18:14.570
his own singing.

00:18:14.570 --> 00:18:18.500
This video is him strutting his stuff as a
filmmaker.

00:18:18.500 --> 00:18:23.020
So while some of it is about The Wall, for
the most part the further you get into it

00:18:23.020 --> 00:18:26.090
the more it shifts to just being about Doug.

00:18:26.090 --> 00:18:31.010
This is a bit of a struggle to describe, which
is why I’m kinda repeating myself over and

00:18:31.010 --> 00:18:33.770
over, but it makes sense when you see it.

00:18:33.770 --> 00:18:35.510
[singing] Tweeting.

00:18:35.510 --> 00:18:39.000
Waiting for the point.

00:18:39.000 --> 00:18:44.070
A bunch of it is just disconnected Weird Al
style parody songs about things that grind

00:18:44.070 --> 00:18:50.000
Doug’s gears, and the parts that are about
The Wall are, more often than not, so off

00:18:50.000 --> 00:18:56.100
base that you would believe the entire thing
was recreated from decades old memories if

00:18:56.100 --> 00:18:59.770
it weren’t for the granular details that
are included.

00:18:59.770 --> 00:19:05.730
It is not Doug Walker’s review of The Wall,
it’s just Doug Walker’s version of The

00:19:05.730 --> 00:19:06.840
Wall.

00:19:06.840 --> 00:19:10.630
So let’s talk about some specifics.

00:19:10.630 --> 00:19:13.447
[singing] Is this quite what you expected
to see?

00:19:13.447 --> 00:19:17.100
Doug’s parody of Another Brick in the Wall
Part 2 is an immediate standout to people

00:19:17.100 --> 00:19:21.870
as just one of the worst things they’ve
put in their brains that they didn’t find

00:19:21.870 --> 00:19:22.870
on rotten.com

00:19:22.870 --> 00:19:25.580
[singing] we need more victimization

00:19:25.580 --> 00:19:32.679
[Dracula voice] there are no good teachers,
not one, not even by accident!

00:19:32.679 --> 00:19:38.970
Restyled as ‘We Need More Victimization’
the song is simultaneously a criticism calling

00:19:38.970 --> 00:19:46.400
The Wall whiney, engaging in bizarre whataboutism,
and a thinly veiled rant about online culture

00:19:46.400 --> 00:19:52.130
war nonsense, a subject that will crop up
a few times throughout the video.

00:19:52.130 --> 00:19:58.529
Most of the substance of this is contained
in Doug’s inexplicable Dracula impression.

00:19:58.529 --> 00:20:02.590
[Dracula voice] Welcome to high school where
all the teachers are monsters!

00:20:02.590 --> 00:20:07.429
I know it looks like they want to teach you
something, but they don’t!

00:20:07.429 --> 00:20:11.919
They just want to eat you up because we’re
evil bloodsucking vampire things!

00:20:11.919 --> 00:20:12.919
Mwahahaha!

00:20:12.919 --> 00:20:17.060
Doug’s argument here is that everyone thinks
school sucks, that that opinion isn’t special

00:20:17.060 --> 00:20:22.710
or noteworthy or substantive, that The Wall
is just dunking on school because it’s a

00:20:22.710 --> 00:20:29.179
popular opinion with teenagers, and, the bizarre
whataboutism of pointing out funding issues

00:20:29.179 --> 00:20:35.030
with the American public education system,
while throwing in some stale jabs about school

00:20:35.030 --> 00:20:37.690
teaching algebra instead of car maintenance.

00:20:37.690 --> 00:20:40.049
So you know what we teach in these walls?

00:20:40.049 --> 00:20:45.510
How to fix a car, pay your taxes, ace an interview,
perform first aid, manage your insurance,

00:20:45.510 --> 00:20:47.360
cook healthy meals, or do household repairs?

00:20:47.360 --> 00:20:48.360
Preposterous!

00:20:48.360 --> 00:20:50.200
We pay other people to do those things.

00:20:50.200 --> 00:20:51.700
We teach important things you need to know
in life, like algebra.

00:20:51.700 --> 00:20:52.700
[singing] oh our education system’s broke

00:20:52.700 --> 00:21:01.780
[Dracula voice] Well, maybe it’s the other
way alound, I don’t know, I’ve got a high

00:21:01.780 --> 00:21:02.780
school education!

00:21:02.780 --> 00:21:03.780
Muahahahah!

00:21:03.780 --> 00:21:04.780
This is pandering like hell

00:21:04.780 --> 00:21:11.380
[singing] L-O-L so school sucks, grow a damn
pair of balls.

00:21:11.380 --> 00:21:17.350
Now, at the surface level here it just needs
to be said, Doug is transparently imposing

00:21:17.350 --> 00:21:23.580
his own experience of school in 1980s 1990s
suburban Illinois on Waters’ experiences

00:21:23.580 --> 00:21:27.820
in Primary School in the 1950s and 60s.

00:21:27.820 --> 00:21:28.820
In England.

00:21:28.820 --> 00:21:32.770
So just at a very fundamental level this doesn’t
work because it’s a complete non-sequitur.

00:21:32.770 --> 00:21:38.279
It’s a response to Another Brick In The
Wall that’s so off topic, that misses the

00:21:38.279 --> 00:21:44.860
mark by such a wide mile, that it’s clear
he just wasn’t paying attention.

00:21:44.860 --> 00:21:49.200
More than that, though, what interests me
here is the way that this version is an argument

00:21:49.200 --> 00:21:51.049
in support of the status quo.

00:21:51.049 --> 00:21:55.630
Let’s talk about the grim cowardice of “school
helps you get a job.”

00:21:55.630 --> 00:21:56.630
[Dracula voice] It’s all part of our seclet
plan to make sure you’re more likely to

00:21:56.630 --> 00:21:59.710
get a job when you’re older!

00:21:59.710 --> 00:22:04.480
Like, okay, let me back up a bit and illustrate
the problem here.

00:22:04.480 --> 00:22:09.610
The roots of public education are split between
two major motivators, the first being philosophical,

00:22:09.610 --> 00:22:11.760
and the second being industrial.

00:22:11.760 --> 00:22:16.270
The philosophical argument is that knowledge
is inherently valuable, and it is a moral

00:22:16.270 --> 00:22:21.679
good for society to educate everyone, that
a society with a widespread familiarity with

00:22:21.679 --> 00:22:26.420
the physical sciences, mathematics, ethics,
literature, and the arts is a more inventive

00:22:26.420 --> 00:22:29.690
society, and a more ethical society.

00:22:29.690 --> 00:22:35.190
The industrial argument is that school should
exist to prepare you to work.

00:22:35.190 --> 00:22:40.179
This is rooted in the real history of much
of public education, as early efforts to expand

00:22:40.179 --> 00:22:46.710
public education were driven by industrialists
whose growing empires needed more specialized

00:22:46.710 --> 00:22:51.290
laborers to do the work of middle management,
which mostly means accounting.

00:22:51.290 --> 00:22:56.200
The idea, from their perspective, was you
could convince the government to teach everyone

00:22:56.200 --> 00:23:00.919
the reading, writing, and arithmetic needed
to do the job, which would make it easier

00:23:00.919 --> 00:23:06.649
to fill all the positions needed, lower training
costs, and drive down the price of math and

00:23:06.649 --> 00:23:08.610
literacy based labour.

00:23:08.610 --> 00:23:13.830
This second argument has had a lot of power
and influence over the shape of education

00:23:13.830 --> 00:23:18.260
in the last two centuries, and is still alive
and well today.

00:23:18.260 --> 00:23:23.279
There is a tremendous amount of ongoing debate
in education circles about the balance between

00:23:23.279 --> 00:23:27.690
raw theory and the pragmatic modern reality
of labour.

00:23:27.690 --> 00:23:32.770
There’s tons of people who will argue that
we should really have two school tracks, so

00:23:32.770 --> 00:23:37.890
that the students who just aren’t that interested
in math and Shakespeare, who think algebra

00:23:37.890 --> 00:23:43.799
is useless, can go watch Amazon training videos
for a couple years and get right to work in

00:23:43.799 --> 00:23:45.570
the fulfilment warehouse.

00:23:45.570 --> 00:23:50.700
The criticism, which Waters is directly engaging
with in Another Brick in the Wall Part 2,

00:23:50.700 --> 00:23:55.730
is that this arrangement, school as a pipeline
to industry, means that what is taught in

00:23:55.730 --> 00:24:01.909
school and how it is taught will be dictated
by industrialists, by corporations.

00:24:01.909 --> 00:24:09.160
The school system, then, becomes nothing but
a systematic reshaping of children into ideal

00:24:09.160 --> 00:24:14.590
workers: obedient, compliant, and narrowly
trained in only what is needed for the job.

00:24:14.590 --> 00:24:19.909
It is a reduction of people to exploitable
labour, to uniform bricks in a wall.

00:24:19.909 --> 00:24:23.070
It is critical of the fundamental ordering
of society.

00:24:23.070 --> 00:24:28.540
Why should these institutions, rampant with
abuse and steered by corporate ghouls, be

00:24:28.540 --> 00:24:30.840
given so much power over us?

00:24:30.840 --> 00:24:34.919
Why, especially, should they be given so much
power over children?

00:24:34.919 --> 00:24:37.860
This all is part of the multi-layered metaphor
of The Wall.

00:24:37.860 --> 00:24:42.590
There is the autobiographical element, placed
in the specifics of English primary school

00:24:42.590 --> 00:24:47.649
in the 1950s and 60s, and the ways that those
experiences shaped Waters into the adult he

00:24:47.649 --> 00:24:53.970
became, which then serves as a means to criticize
the existence of the system itself, because

00:24:53.970 --> 00:24:59.260
the abuses he suffered, the physical violence
of beating children who failed in the rote

00:24:59.260 --> 00:25:05.429
recitation of number tables, were systematized,
and took place within a system that was at

00:25:05.429 --> 00:25:09.110
best indifferent and at worst approving.

00:25:09.110 --> 00:25:13.309
This continues to resonate with people to
this day because regardless of whether or

00:25:13.309 --> 00:25:19.519
not corporal punishment has been abolished
and more idealistic pedagogies have prevailed,

00:25:19.519 --> 00:25:25.649
institutionalized school is still an institution
and will never be an unalloyed good.

00:25:25.649 --> 00:25:31.100
It will always be vulnerable to corruption,
to abuse, to manipulation, and the question

00:25:31.100 --> 00:25:38.000
of whose agenda is served by the structure
and material must remain an eternal debate.

00:25:38.000 --> 00:25:44.970
So, yeah, I feel confident saying that Doug’s
argument here just isn’t very well formed,

00:25:44.970 --> 00:25:48.920
it’s unclear, shallow, and doesn’t actually
engage with the question.

00:25:48.920 --> 00:25:50.020
D minus.

00:25:50.020 --> 00:25:54.850
Wait time out, is this really a World War
2 reference?

00:25:54.850 --> 00:25:58.830
No, it’s just a bunch of kids being herded
onto a train to a horrible torturous building,

00:25:58.830 --> 00:25:59.830
ooooh

00:25:59.830 --> 00:26:03.419
Yeah, and we’re really comparing this to
high school?

00:26:03.419 --> 00:26:07.690
Following “We Need More Victimization”
is an extended skit where Doug, dressed as

00:26:07.690 --> 00:26:10.559
The Critic, defends The Wall from criticism.

00:26:10.559 --> 00:26:14.409
No, no, it’s just meant to look weird and
creepy.

00:26:14.409 --> 00:26:15.950
They’re not implying… that.

00:26:15.950 --> 00:26:20.710
The Critic is positioned as the rube, his
defences are clearly meant to be hollow and

00:26:20.710 --> 00:26:21.710
weak.

00:26:21.710 --> 00:26:26.809
The problem is that the accusations he’s
sarcastically defending the movie against

00:26:26.809 --> 00:26:33.769
only hold weight based on Doug’s own shallow
misreading of everything else up to this point.

00:26:33.769 --> 00:26:38.779
In The Wall Pink, as a child, hiding in a
train tunnel, sees schoolchildren as faceless

00:26:38.779 --> 00:26:44.380
blobs crowded into cattle cars, and envisions
himself, too, becoming faceless.

00:26:44.380 --> 00:26:47.930
This is the beginning of ‘The Happiest Days
of Our Lives’ which is effectively just

00:26:47.930 --> 00:26:52.539
the intro to Another Brick in the Wall Part
2, during which the faceless children are

00:26:52.539 --> 00:26:54.630
fed into a meat grinder.

00:26:54.630 --> 00:26:58.309
So the more immediate imagery is a slaughterhouse.

00:26:58.309 --> 00:27:03.510
Is that, in part or by extension, also a Holocaust
reference?

00:27:03.510 --> 00:27:04.850
Yeah, it is.

00:27:04.850 --> 00:27:07.210
It is a “world war 2 reference.”

00:27:07.210 --> 00:27:12.500
Because much of the album and movie are about
the after effects of that war.

00:27:12.500 --> 00:27:17.440
It’s not an abstract concept that’s just
being name-dropped for aesthetic symmetry,

00:27:17.440 --> 00:27:23.919
it’s a real event that people lived through,
and The Wall is in no small part a reflection

00:27:23.919 --> 00:27:30.180
on growing up in the shadow of that and reflecting
on the value of it, or more specifically the

00:27:30.180 --> 00:27:31.889
lack thereof.

00:27:31.889 --> 00:27:37.809
The statement is that the inhumanity of the
system, its goals and methods, are ultimately

00:27:37.809 --> 00:27:45.610
homoousian, of the same stuff, as the inhumanity
that leads a system to load humans into cattle

00:27:45.610 --> 00:27:46.610
cars.

00:27:46.610 --> 00:27:52.400
That comparison is only inappropriate if,
one, you think the message is simply “school

00:27:52.400 --> 00:27:57.549
sucks”, and two, you’ve been ignoring
the role of World War 2 within the film up

00:27:57.549 --> 00:27:58.549
to this point.

00:27:58.549 --> 00:27:59.549
Oh come on, they’re just saying school sucks,
they’re not making any connections to that.

00:27:59.549 --> 00:28:05.640
Now let’s go on to the next song that talks
about eh horrors of World War 2, ah crap.

00:28:05.640 --> 00:28:06.640
See.

00:28:06.640 --> 00:28:08.399
It’s just a coincidence!

00:28:08.399 --> 00:28:09.399
Maybe?

00:28:09.399 --> 00:28:11.419
I don’t know.

00:28:11.419 --> 00:28:15.920
As those of you familiar with The Wall may
have noticed, this is all out of order because

00:28:15.920 --> 00:28:18.760
the joke doesn’t work if you do it in order.

00:28:18.760 --> 00:28:24.720
Doug’s joke is a setup for his version of
Goodbye Blue Sky, retooled as So Long, Weird

00:28:24.720 --> 00:28:25.720
Song.

00:28:25.720 --> 00:28:29.059
[singing] D-d-d-d-doesn’t this seem too
heavy?

00:28:29.059 --> 00:28:34.010
Is World War 2 with monsters too silly?

00:28:34.010 --> 00:28:36.030
Originally this is all the other way around.

00:28:36.030 --> 00:28:40.740
Goodbye Blue Sky leads into the train tunnel
which segues into Another Brick pt. 2.

00:28:40.740 --> 00:28:46.940
The reason that matters is because originally
the imagery of Goodbye Blue Sky is the setup

00:28:46.940 --> 00:28:48.290
for later imagery.

00:28:48.290 --> 00:28:53.269
Gerald Scarfe’s powerful visualization of
the War and specifically The Blitz as a psychological

00:28:53.269 --> 00:28:59.010
force that scarred the collective psyche of
the people as deeply as it scarred the land

00:28:59.010 --> 00:29:00.710
and the bodies of soldiers.

00:29:00.710 --> 00:29:08.220
Like, I cannot stress this enough, Goodbye
Blue Sky is one of the most potent pieces

00:29:08.220 --> 00:29:10.360
of filmmaking I’ve ever seen.

00:29:10.360 --> 00:29:17.210
It is ruinously effective at using its imagery
to convey the sheer scale of damage and waste

00:29:17.210 --> 00:29:18.860
that the war represents.

00:29:18.860 --> 00:29:24.650
It’s grim, and haunting, and very important
as a foundation for what follows.

00:29:24.650 --> 00:29:30.520
In ‘So Long, Weird Song’ Doug asks if
“world war 2 with monsters” is too silly

00:29:30.520 --> 00:29:34.640
for a movie that also complains about how
High School bites.”

00:29:34.640 --> 00:29:37.740
[singing] D-d-d-d-doesn’t this seem too
heavy?

00:29:37.740 --> 00:29:43.460
Is World War 2 with monsters too silly?

00:29:43.460 --> 00:29:56.410
Oh Roger Waters did you ever wonder why these
images of slaughter made it in a film that

00:29:56.410 --> 00:29:57.410
also sings about how high school bites?

00:29:57.410 --> 00:30:00.590
Which, of course, again, is predicated on
that actually being the message, which it

00:30:00.590 --> 00:30:05.720
isn’t, and indulges in a deeply misplaced
level of literalism by describing the sequence

00:30:05.720 --> 00:30:11.700
as “world war 2 with monsters” as though
this were a direct-to-video Pacific Rim sequel.

00:30:11.700 --> 00:30:16.030
Now, this kind of literalism is a pretty regular
part of Doug’s normal bit, it just really

00:30:16.030 --> 00:30:17.269
falls apart here.

00:30:17.269 --> 00:30:21.390
It’s one thing when it’s aimed at corporate
franchise schlock aimed at kids, but it’s

00:30:21.390 --> 00:30:27.890
just really obviously misplaced and inappropriate
when applied to a deeply earnest piece of

00:30:27.890 --> 00:30:33.920
art that is overtly symbolic, unapologetically
symbolic in its attempt to communicate what

00:30:33.920 --> 00:30:39.250
this real event, that really happened, felt
like to live through.

00:30:39.250 --> 00:30:44.500
He also calls the song “Oscar bait”, which
is funny because it’s both literally wrong,

00:30:44.500 --> 00:30:48.649
Goodbye Blue Sky wasn’t eligible for an
Oscar in the first place, and also because

00:30:48.649 --> 00:30:52.510
it’s such a deep insult that it’s really
revealing.

00:30:52.510 --> 00:30:53.510
[singing] So long Oscar bait song.

00:30:53.510 --> 00:31:00.350
Smoke a bong and it’ll feel less wrong.

00:31:00.350 --> 00:31:06.620
Now, I actually don’t think Doug was literally
calling the song “Oscar bait”, I don’t

00:31:06.620 --> 00:31:11.929
think he thinks they were specifically playing
towards the Academy, but I do think that he

00:31:11.929 --> 00:31:17.769
thinks The Wall is spiritually Oscar bait,
which is to say insincere.

00:31:17.769 --> 00:31:23.539
The capstone here, for me, is the replacement
animation, which is, first of all, just visually

00:31:23.539 --> 00:31:30.440
awful and cheap in a way that would be considered
sub-par for 2007, and second, is just so petty

00:31:30.440 --> 00:31:36.830
and mean that this is what makes it very funny
when Doug calls his album a “love letter

00:31:36.830 --> 00:31:38.039
to Pink Floyd”.

00:31:38.039 --> 00:31:45.399
The imperial eagle is labeled Ego, and the
frightened ones are labeled self-content?

00:31:45.399 --> 00:31:46.399
Self-content?

00:31:46.399 --> 00:31:51.950
I honestly can’t tell if that’s supposed
to be content or content because neither really

00:31:51.950 --> 00:31:53.399
makes sense?

00:31:53.399 --> 00:31:57.600
I think he’s saying ego is the destroyer
of self-content?

00:31:57.600 --> 00:32:03.380
Like, Roger Waters’ dissatisfaction is not
the result of any real problems, but is a

00:32:03.380 --> 00:32:06.250
product of his own ego?

00:32:06.250 --> 00:32:12.190
That would parse, because the lyrics of the
song do directly address Roger Waters, and

00:32:12.190 --> 00:32:18.389
later a cloud labeled “my problems” grows
flowers of pity and then blasts another cloud

00:32:18.389 --> 00:32:25.840
labeled “other people’s problems.”

00:32:25.840 --> 00:32:37.460
[singing] ooooooh oooooh ooooooh oooooooh

00:32:37.460 --> 00:32:41.799
I dunno, I think it’s one thing to describe
The Wall as whiney or juvenile, to say in

00:32:41.799 --> 00:32:47.930
effect that it encapsulates a really teenage
mindset, but it’s quite another to accuse

00:32:47.930 --> 00:32:54.820
the creators of being disingenuous, ego-tripping
fakes who milk imaginary problems for pity.

00:32:54.820 --> 00:32:59.820
I don’t think you can make that accusation
and then turn around and insist that you liked

00:32:59.820 --> 00:33:03.200
it fine, and made a love letter to its creative
vision.

00:33:03.200 --> 00:33:08.350
That’s the kind of thing that you say about
something you just really don’t think very

00:33:08.350 --> 00:33:10.649
highly of at all.

00:33:10.649 --> 00:33:28.700
[singing] That’s why I watch, despite it
being so whiny, ya see.

00:33:28.700 --> 00:33:30.429
[singing] This is pandering like hell

00:33:30.429 --> 00:33:31.429
[singing] So long Oscar bait song

00:33:31.429 --> 00:33:32.429
[singing] You’re not Oscar bound

00:33:32.429 --> 00:33:34.019
This is such a love letter to Pink Floyd’s
music and The Wall

00:33:34.019 --> 00:33:39.420
The song Comfortably Numb, retooled as Comfortably
Dumb, is where things start to really wander

00:33:39.420 --> 00:33:40.420
off topic.

00:33:40.420 --> 00:33:45.029
It’s not really about The Wall but it’s
kinda about Doug’s relationship to The Wall,

00:33:45.029 --> 00:33:50.660
and to media in general, and it’s probably
the most honest song Doug put into the project,

00:33:50.660 --> 00:33:52.640
almost certainly not on purpose.

00:33:52.640 --> 00:33:57.269
Nominally it’s about the pacing of the film,
and Doug reiterates once again that he thinks

00:33:57.269 --> 00:34:02.940
Roger Waters’ opinions are the illegitimate
whining of a millionaire, but it’s also

00:34:02.940 --> 00:34:06.799
Doug saying he doesn’t understand and he
doesn’t care to.

00:34:06.799 --> 00:34:11.429
The movie is challenging and he just doesn’t
have any interest in engaging with it on its

00:34:11.429 --> 00:34:12.929
own terms.

00:34:12.929 --> 00:34:15.970
He has become comfortably dumb.

00:34:15.970 --> 00:34:28.179
[singing] I just don’t understand, is this
now how I am?

00:34:28.179 --> 00:34:33.720
I have become comfortably dumb.

00:34:33.720 --> 00:34:39.460
And, you know what, I’m not going to disagree.

00:34:39.460 --> 00:34:40.460
You said it, man.

00:34:40.460 --> 00:34:45.530
I don’t know if it works as self-deprecating
humour because I don’t think it’s comedically

00:34:45.530 --> 00:34:47.960
true, I think it’s just true.

00:34:47.960 --> 00:34:50.389
Going into the ad break there’s a changeover.

00:34:50.389 --> 00:34:56.280
So, okay, up to this point Corey Taylor, the
frontman for nu metal band Slipknot, has been

00:34:56.280 --> 00:34:59.569
playing the equivalent role of Pink.

00:34:59.569 --> 00:35:04.690
As they transition Corey is replaced with
Doug, so Doug plays Pink for the equivalents

00:35:04.690 --> 00:35:07.120
of In The Flesh and Waiting for the Worms.

00:35:07.120 --> 00:35:10.890
This is part of a running gag, being that
the actual rock musician doesn’t get to

00:35:10.890 --> 00:35:15.820
sing a song until the very end at which point
he plays the Spongebob Squarepants theme,

00:35:15.820 --> 00:35:20.849
and also probably just a logistical extension
of only getting Corey out for a day or two

00:35:20.849 --> 00:35:22.950
and block shooting all his stuff.

00:35:22.950 --> 00:35:28.370
The other thing that’s going on, which is
far more trivial but bothers me so much, is

00:35:28.370 --> 00:35:33.819
that the exterior shot is overwhelmingly blue,
and I’m not sure if this is a stylistic

00:35:33.819 --> 00:35:39.300
choice or a mistake, that they just forgot
to set the white balance properly and just

00:35:39.300 --> 00:35:40.300
left it.

00:35:40.300 --> 00:35:43.290
Because Doug does that, just, all the time.

00:35:43.290 --> 00:35:49.109
After the ad break, which consists of an uncomfortably
over-acted ad for online data harvesting scam

00:35:49.109 --> 00:35:54.950
Honey and Doug’s own tie-in recreation of
the entire album The Wall, which, again, they

00:35:54.950 --> 00:35:57.079
were selling for money, we get this.

00:35:57.079 --> 00:36:11.490
[Music: ‘In The Flesh’]

00:36:11.490 --> 00:36:12.900
Truly every frame is a painting.

00:36:12.900 --> 00:36:17.140
[singing] Well I’m that nameless foe, ha!

00:36:17.140 --> 00:36:21.331
No, okay, so, mm, where do I start with this?

00:36:21.331 --> 00:36:22.331
[singing] I got some weird news for you sunshine.

00:36:22.331 --> 00:36:25.000
This was dissin’ Thatcher’s administration.

00:36:25.000 --> 00:36:32.700
But it’s vague enough to put anyone you
feel.

00:36:32.700 --> 00:36:38.690
Politician, showman, just put their face here!

00:36:38.690 --> 00:36:41.150
No, no doug.

00:36:41.150 --> 00:36:42.170
No.

00:36:42.170 --> 00:36:44.329
No, it really wasn’t.

00:36:44.329 --> 00:36:49.300
[singing] Are there any queers in the audience
tonight?

00:36:49.300 --> 00:36:50.869
It really, it really, really wasn’t that
vague.

00:36:50.869 --> 00:36:54.490
[singing] That one looks Jewish, and that
one’s a coon!

00:36:54.490 --> 00:36:56.190
Not vague at all.

00:36:56.190 --> 00:37:03.010
[singing] Get ‘em up against the wall!

00:37:03.010 --> 00:37:04.010
(Against!

00:37:04.010 --> 00:37:05.010
The!

00:37:05.010 --> 00:37:06.010
Wall!)

00:37:06.010 --> 00:37:07.010
Not even a teeny tiny bit vague.

00:37:07.010 --> 00:37:10.010
[Doug singing] That one’s looking stressed,
he wants to feel oppressed.

00:37:10.010 --> 00:37:14.160
Put him in the wall!

00:37:14.160 --> 00:37:15.160
(Post!)

00:37:15.160 --> 00:37:19.750
Okay, so this whole segment is just Doug venting
about people being mean to him on Twitter.

00:37:19.750 --> 00:37:24.800
It is completely disconnected from The Wall,
not even a tiny bit about the movie, it’s

00:37:24.800 --> 00:37:30.650
just Doug using the veil of alter ego to express
an otherwise authentically held belief.

00:37:30.650 --> 00:37:35.359
And that’s honestly a charitable reading
of it.

00:37:35.359 --> 00:37:43.920
Because otherwise you’re saying that a criticism
of Thatcherism, the policies of austerity,

00:37:43.920 --> 00:37:48.520
of rigid enforcement of Christian norms, the
violent suppression of political dissent,

00:37:48.520 --> 00:37:55.200
of a rising home-grown fascist movement, criticisms
that pre-date the AIDS crisis but would hold

00:37:55.200 --> 00:38:02.180
true well into the 80s as governments decided
it was unofficial policy that AIDS was a divine

00:38:02.180 --> 00:38:10.790
punishment and a solution to their “gay
crisis,” you’re saying that all that isn’t

00:38:10.790 --> 00:38:16.079
substantively different from people yelling
at each other on the internet over Batman

00:38:16.079 --> 00:38:17.079
movies.

00:38:17.079 --> 00:38:21.310
[singing] that one looks like he really wants
to be outraged!

00:38:21.310 --> 00:38:26.510
Now with social media you have the stage!

00:38:26.510 --> 00:38:35.140
So, yeah, I think the most charitable read
is that Doug’s just talking about himself.

00:38:35.140 --> 00:38:37.450
[chanting] Hashtag!

00:38:37.450 --> 00:38:38.609
Hashtag!

00:38:38.609 --> 00:38:39.770
Hashtag!

00:38:39.770 --> 00:38:40.930
Hashtag!

00:38:40.930 --> 00:38:42.089
Hashtag!

00:38:42.089 --> 00:38:49.460
Alright, there’s only one major section
left and this is maybe where I risk a controversial

00:38:49.460 --> 00:38:50.820
opinion.

00:38:50.820 --> 00:38:55.770
Common among reactions to this video is to
say that while the review as a whole is really

00:38:55.770 --> 00:39:00.910
bad, the animation at the end is really good,
it’s the best part, and anyone who is criticizing

00:39:00.910 --> 00:39:06.540
the review should, you know, be kind to Fennah,
the animator who put it all together, because

00:39:06.540 --> 00:39:08.609
it was a lot of work.

00:39:08.609 --> 00:39:15.190
So what happens is Corey enters the Channel
Awesome office and is about to confront Doug

00:39:15.190 --> 00:39:17.960
when Doug is interrupted by a phone call.

00:39:17.960 --> 00:39:24.230
Or is it just a misguided ego trip into self-indulgent
world that we all live in, pardon!

00:39:24.230 --> 00:39:25.420
Who is it?!

00:39:25.420 --> 00:39:26.609
Hello there, critic.

00:39:26.609 --> 00:39:27.810
It’s Sullivan Croft.

00:39:27.810 --> 00:39:29.350
The Satellite City guy?

00:39:29.350 --> 00:39:30.420
Yes, actually.

00:39:30.420 --> 00:39:34.520
Um, I was, uh, is Lucy Lacemaker there?

00:39:34.520 --> 00:39:35.520
Is she there?

00:39:35.520 --> 00:39:36.520
‘Cus I was, ah, it’s just a thing

00:39:36.520 --> 00:39:38.900
[crosstalk] S-sullivan, have you found her
yet?

00:39:38.900 --> 00:39:40.900
Uh, shut the fuck up.

00:39:40.900 --> 00:39:45.589
Now, I’ve done all the research to piece
all this together so that I can summarize

00:39:45.589 --> 00:39:50.829
it, but on first viewing I had zero clue what
was going on.

00:39:50.829 --> 00:39:56.690
I don’t know who this is, but it’s clearly
a reference that the video expects me to get,

00:39:56.690 --> 00:40:02.150
there’s clearly some sort of backstory to
these characters, it’s not just an abstraction,

00:40:02.150 --> 00:40:08.630
and just to really add to the confusion the
whole thing is this mumblecore overlapping

00:40:08.630 --> 00:40:12.700
ramble, and it looks like garbage, and you
can’t understand what they’re talking

00:40:12.700 --> 00:40:15.720
about because you can’t understand what
they’re even saying.

00:40:15.720 --> 00:40:20.069
You’re not exactly giving creation all of
its credit, it’s not like you just shit

00:40:20.069 --> 00:40:23.960
out the Horcrux that Voldemort never meant
to make.

00:40:23.960 --> 00:40:29.710
And then this thing shows up and the barely
intelligible dialogue just keeps going until

00:40:29.710 --> 00:40:36.309
the whole thing segues into The Trial where
it honestly doesn’t get much easier to understand

00:40:36.309 --> 00:40:40.819
[singing] Lazy.

00:40:40.819 --> 00:40:45.329
Constantly ???????????????? Lazy

00:40:45.329 --> 00:40:51.990
Now, before we get into this any further,
yes, there’s a lot of technical competency

00:40:51.990 --> 00:40:53.329
on display.

00:40:53.329 --> 00:40:58.670
Modelling is hard, texturing is hard, rigging
is hard, animation is hard, hair simulations

00:40:58.670 --> 00:41:03.930
are hard, all of this represents hours and
hours and hours and hours of work.

00:41:03.930 --> 00:41:06.990
There is a lot of effort on display here.

00:41:06.990 --> 00:41:07.990
Okay.

00:41:07.990 --> 00:41:13.211
So, Fennah is a YouTube animator who makes
a series called Satellite City which is, I

00:41:13.211 --> 00:41:20.280
guess, a skit show with a combination of live
action and CG animation and way, way, way

00:41:20.280 --> 00:41:25.510
too much lore for a series with an average
episode runtime of one hundred and thirty

00:41:25.510 --> 00:41:26.720
seconds.

00:41:26.720 --> 00:41:32.890
So the characters in The Trial are all characters
from Satellite City, and it’s just, it’s

00:41:32.890 --> 00:41:34.630
not good.

00:41:34.630 --> 00:41:39.420
Kinda the real change of pace is that it’s
not good in ways that are new to the review

00:41:39.420 --> 00:41:40.579
so far.

00:41:40.579 --> 00:41:44.391
From what I can glean this segment was made
by Fennah more or less in isolation from the

00:41:44.391 --> 00:41:45.670
rest of the video.

00:41:45.670 --> 00:41:49.510
The track on the album is called Fennah’s
The Trial, the credits say it’s produced

00:41:49.510 --> 00:41:54.730
by Fennah, and so it does seem like Fennah
wrote and produced all of it, using his normal

00:41:54.730 --> 00:41:56.770
stable of voice actors.

00:41:56.770 --> 00:42:01.840
Which makes sense because the opinions are
bad, but they’re a different kind of bad.

00:42:01.840 --> 00:42:07.150
The entire song is sung in-universe from the
viewpoint of these characters who feel that

00:42:07.150 --> 00:42:11.550
The Wall falls short because all the animated
monsters in the movie didn’t get enough

00:42:11.550 --> 00:42:17.250
screen time or development, and then another
character comes in and says that, you know,

00:42:17.250 --> 00:42:20.740
I guess we can give it a pass because it wasn’t
trying to tell a story.

00:42:20.740 --> 00:42:35.349
[singing] This film's not bound by characters
It's a visual affair It never aimed to tell

00:42:35.349 --> 00:42:38.180
a story Rather, a sprawling canvas of emotion,
visual splendor

00:42:38.180 --> 00:42:39.520
Okay, so this sucks.

00:42:39.520 --> 00:42:45.619
That’s just a really weak, disingenuous
argument being filtered through a character

00:42:45.619 --> 00:42:52.160
in order to scrub it of any meaning or ownership,
because heaven forbid you put an actual opinion

00:42:52.160 --> 00:42:53.290
out into the world.

00:42:53.290 --> 00:42:56.550
It’s also just wrong at a very basic level.

00:42:56.550 --> 00:42:58.750
The Wall is telling a story.

00:42:58.750 --> 00:43:04.150
It’s not conventionally told, and it’s
a little loose, but it’s very comprehensible.

00:43:04.150 --> 00:43:09.840
The animated monsters at the end of the movie
aren’t under-developed in the slightest,

00:43:09.840 --> 00:43:15.980
because they’re extensions and representations
of characters that were already present in

00:43:15.980 --> 00:43:17.480
the rest of the movie.

00:43:17.480 --> 00:43:24.140
They are a psychosexual visualization of those
literal characters and the ideas those characters

00:43:24.140 --> 00:43:28.480
represent: parents, the state, the education
system, intimate partners.

00:43:28.480 --> 00:43:33.869
The problem with using Fennah’s horny edgelord
steampunk furry OCs is that they actually

00:43:33.869 --> 00:43:35.309
are underdeveloped.

00:43:35.309 --> 00:43:39.730
If there’s even an attempt at using them
in a metaphorical fashion then that’s lost

00:43:39.730 --> 00:43:42.609
in the clear specificity of the crossover.

00:43:42.609 --> 00:43:47.930
They’re not present in any meaningful capacity
through the rest of the review, they don’t

00:43:47.930 --> 00:43:52.980
represent anything substantive, and they are,
for the most part, awful over-baked designs

00:43:52.980 --> 00:43:54.900
for one-note characters.

00:43:54.900 --> 00:43:59.430
fills me with the urge to decapitate!

00:43:59.430 --> 00:44:04.090
So you’re going to have this over-designed
horny rat thing lecture about how The Wall

00:44:04.090 --> 00:44:10.190
is all style over substance, devoid of intellectual
development, then alien big titty goth girlfriend

00:44:10.190 --> 00:44:11.809
calls the movie lazy?

00:44:11.809 --> 00:44:19.109
And when I heard you were reviewing the much-to-be-desired
Wall I figured I’d find my own tuppence.

00:44:19.109 --> 00:44:24.750
Style over substance, showcasing a bunch of
weird creatures and worlds, not a drop of

00:44:24.750 --> 00:44:26.780
intellectual development.

00:44:26.780 --> 00:44:29.990
[singing] Lazy.

00:44:29.990 --> 00:44:33.200
Constantly ???????????????? Lazy

00:44:33.200 --> 00:44:34.810
Cringe.

00:44:34.810 --> 00:44:37.670
There’s no other word for it.

00:44:37.670 --> 00:44:39.090
This makes me cringe.

00:44:39.090 --> 00:44:40.160
It’s embarrassing.

00:44:40.160 --> 00:44:43.010
On a deep level the entire structure of this
just doesn’t work.

00:44:43.010 --> 00:44:47.300
It’s poor criticism and it’s not even
an appropriate parallel to the segment that

00:44:47.300 --> 00:44:49.119
it’s a recreation of.

00:44:49.119 --> 00:44:54.390
If this were actually a coherent work this
isn’t where you would put the movie on trial,

00:44:54.390 --> 00:44:56.910
this is where you would put Doug on trial.

00:44:56.910 --> 00:45:00.990
This is where you would take all the other
ideas from the rest of the review, consolidate

00:45:00.990 --> 00:45:05.540
them in one place, interrogate them, and interrogate
what they say about their author.

00:45:05.540 --> 00:45:09.640
So, no, I don’t think this is the “good
part” of the review, because it’s still

00:45:09.640 --> 00:45:15.200
a tremendous amount of effort put into modelling,
texturing, and rigging a fundamentally bad

00:45:15.200 --> 00:45:16.200
concept.

00:45:16.200 --> 00:45:19.040
Just at the basics it’s confusing and muddled.

00:45:19.040 --> 00:45:23.930
Creatively it seems to have started from the
ending, the chant of Tear Down The Wall, taken

00:45:23.930 --> 00:45:29.190
from the original and recontextualized to
mean tear down The Wall, the movie, and working

00:45:29.190 --> 00:45:35.359
backwards from that is a bunch of stuff just
hammered into place because if you’re doing

00:45:35.359 --> 00:45:38.720
a full recreation then you’ve gotta put
something in there.

00:45:38.720 --> 00:45:42.109
You’ve got a lyric sheet to fill out and
time to kill.

00:45:42.109 --> 00:45:46.220
And of course, afterwards, the whole review
ends with a lazy lampshade of the fact that

00:45:46.220 --> 00:45:49.740
the review didn’t actually contain anything
of substance.

00:45:49.740 --> 00:45:50.740
Okay!

00:45:50.740 --> 00:45:51.740
I liked it fine.

00:45:51.740 --> 00:45:52.740
Yeah, me too.

00:45:52.740 --> 00:45:53.970
A little full of itself, but good music and
imagination.

00:45:53.970 --> 00:45:54.970
Fair enough.

00:45:54.970 --> 00:45:59.240
And the resolution of the gag that Corey Taylor
hasn’t done anything yet in the form of

00:45:59.240 --> 00:46:01.750
the Spongebob Squarepants theme song.

00:46:01.750 --> 00:46:03.570
[singing] Spongebob Squarepants!

00:46:03.570 --> 00:46:08.420
Kinda the buckwild thing here is that if there
were less effort put into this, if the substance

00:46:08.420 --> 00:46:15.230
was otherwise exactly the same but there wasn’t
a companion album on sale for money, there

00:46:15.230 --> 00:46:21.369
wasn’t an entire end-to-end abridged recreation,
if there were just one or two songs and a

00:46:21.369 --> 00:46:26.320
bunch of lazy jokes about the movie being
really pretentious, then I don’t think this

00:46:26.320 --> 00:46:28.880
would compel people in the way it does.

00:46:28.880 --> 00:46:34.790
There’s an effort on display that is its
own form of earnestness, and it just all falls

00:46:34.790 --> 00:46:37.309
flat, because it’s just no good.

00:46:37.309 --> 00:46:40.820
It’s trying to be impressive without being
vulnerable.

00:46:40.820 --> 00:46:45.910
And simultaneous to that it’s pissing on
a really earnest piece of art specifically

00:46:45.910 --> 00:46:48.000
for being vulnerable.

00:46:48.000 --> 00:46:52.839
And to see this all played out against the
backdrop of a movie from a band that is decades

00:46:52.839 --> 00:46:58.510
and decades and decades old, well outside
Doug’s normal wheelhouse, with no clear

00:46:58.510 --> 00:47:06.050
indication of why this particular movie, this
album, is something that Doug feel is worth

00:47:06.050 --> 00:47:10.210
this much effort, it’s a baffling product.

00:47:10.210 --> 00:47:12.240
[Hat!Dan] But wait!

00:47:12.240 --> 00:47:13.240
Holy crap!

00:47:13.240 --> 00:47:16.940
It’s long running character Hat!Dan, the
Dan With a Hat!

00:47:16.940 --> 00:47:19.170
What did you really think of it?!

00:47:19.170 --> 00:47:20.170
I liked it fine.

00:47:20.170 --> 00:47:22.780
A little full of itself but they put in a
lot of effort.

00:47:22.780 --> 00:47:23.780
yeah!

00:47:23.780 --> 00:47:24.780
Yeah!

00:47:24.780 --> 00:47:25.280
[gunshots]

